/-! # The integer residue model at a closed prime

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 07PJ, proof of case (3), checking condition (4)
of Tag 07PC at a nonzero prime (blueprint: lem-trf-integers-closed-residue-model).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

namespace Int

/-- The residue field at `(p)` is the quotient of the integers by `(p)`, compatibly with the
canonical maps. Every finite purely inseparable extension is already the image of the integers;
this specified image is a finite Noetherian regular model, generated over the integers by `1`.
The binder `B` and its equality simply name the image subring of the specified composite.

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 07PJ, proof of case (3), checking condition (4)
of Tag 07PC at a nonzero prime (blueprint: lem-trf-integers-closed-residue-model).

Proof:
1. `lem-weil-pbc-z-localizations-dvr` identifies `(p)` as prime and the maximal ideal of the
   localization as the ideal generated by `p`. By `def-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime`, residue
   classes are represented by fractions `a/s` with `p` not dividing `s`. Choose the least
   positive integer linear combination `d = u*s + v*p`. Division with remainder shows that
   `d` divides both `s` and `p`, since a nonzero remainder would be a smaller positive linear
   combination. Primality and `p` not dividing `s` imply `d = 1`. The identity
   `a/s - a*u = a*v*p/s` shows that every residue class comes from an integer.
2. The kernel contains `(p)`. If an integer `t` maps to zero, write `t/1 = p*a/s` with
   `p` not dividing `s`. Equality of fractions gives `w*(s*t-p*a) = 0` for `w` not divisible
   by `p`; the domain property of the integers gives `s*t = p*a`. With `u*s + v*p = 1` as
   above, `t = p*(u*a+v*t)`. Thus the kernel is exactly `(p)`. The induced quotient map
   `[a] ↦ [a/1]` is bijective and a ring map, so its inverse also preserves the ring
   operations. This gives the compatible ring isomorphism. In particular `p` vanishes in
   the residue field and in every extension field.
3. For the finite purely inseparable extension, `def-weil-ring-frobenius` gives the ring
   endomorphism `F^n(x) = x^(p^n)`. It fixes integer multiples of `1`, hence every element
   of the image of the residue field by step 1. The characteristic-positive clause of
   `def-weil-pbc-finite-purely-inseparable-extension` gives `x^(p^n) = j(a)` for some `n,a`.
   Therefore `(x-j(a))^(p^n) = 0`. Since the exponent is positive and the extension is a
   field, `x = j(a)`. This proves surjectivity, including when `n = 0`.
4. The composite of the two surjections has image the whole field, so the prescribed `B`
   is the top subring. Each element of `B` is the image of an integer `m`, hence is `m • 1`.
   Thus `{1}` spans `B`, giving finiteness in `def-weil-finitely-generated-module`.
   Every element of the extension field is the fraction `x/1` of elements of `B`.
5. Apply `lem-weil-pbc-field-regular-ring` to the field identified with `B`: it is Noetherian
   and every prime localization is regular local, exactly the ring clauses of
   `def-weil-pbc-noetherian-ring` and `def-weil-regular-scheme`. By
   `def-weil-affine-spectrum` its spectrum has a point. The whole spectrum is open and
   regular, giving the asserted nonempty regular open subset. -/
theorem integers_closed_residue_model (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] :
    Function.Surjective (algebraMap ℤ (Ideal.span {(p : ℤ)}).ResidueField) ∧
      RingHom.ker (algebraMap ℤ (Ideal.span {(p : ℤ)}).ResidueField) =
        Ideal.span {(p : ℤ)} ∧
      (∃ e : (ℤ ⧸ Ideal.span {(p : ℤ)}) ≃+* (Ideal.span {(p : ℤ)}).ResidueField,
        ∀ a : ℤ, e (Ideal.Quotient.mk (Ideal.span {(p : ℤ)}) a) =
          algebraMap ℤ (Ideal.span {(p : ℤ)}).ResidueField a) ∧
      ∀ (L : Type u) [Field L] [Algebra (Ideal.span {(p : ℤ)}).ResidueField L]
        [Module.Finite (Ideal.span {(p : ℤ)}).ResidueField L]
        [IsPurelyInseparable (Ideal.span {(p : ℤ)}).ResidueField L],
        Function.Surjective (algebraMap (Ideal.span {(p : ℤ)}).ResidueField L) ∧
          ∀ B : Subring L,
            B = ((algebraMap (Ideal.span {(p : ℤ)}).ResidueField L).comp
              (algebraMap ℤ (Ideal.span {(p : ℤ)}).ResidueField)).range →
            B = ⊤ ∧ IsNoetherianRing B ∧ Module.Finite ℤ B ∧
              Submodule.span ℤ ({(1 : B)} : Set B) = ⊤ ∧
              (∀ x : L, ∃ b c : B, (c : L) ≠ 0 ∧ x = (b : L) / (c : L)) ∧
              Nonempty (AlgebraicGeometry.Spec (CommRingCat.of B)) ∧
              (∀ (q : Ideal B) [q.IsPrime], IsRegularLocalRing (Localization.AtPrime q)) ∧
              AlgebraicGeometry.IsRegularScheme (AlgebraicGeometry.Spec (CommRingCat.of B)) := by
  sorry

end Int
